---------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2606.03046] ZK-Flex: A Flexible and Scalable Framework for Accelerating Zero-Knowledge Proofs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.03046)\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.03046\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 163\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Hardware Architecture\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 2 Jun 2026]\nL9: # Title:ZK-Flex: A Flexible and Scalable Framework for Accelerating Zero-Knowledge Proofs\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Adiwena Putra】, 【6†Cuong Manh Duong】, 【7†Anh Quang Pham】, 【8†Joo-Young Kim】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled ZK-Flex: A Flexible and Scalable Framework for Accelerating Zero-Knowledge Proofs, by Adiwena Putra and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【9†View PDF】 【10†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Zero-knowledge proofs (ZKP) allows a prover to convince a verifier of computational correctness without revealing private data, ensuring both privacy and verifiability. However, proof generation is highly compute-intensive, dominated by polynomial (POLY) and elliptic-curve (EC) operations.\nL17: These workloads pose two key challenges for hardware acceleration: (1) efficiently supporting diverse large-precision modular multiplications, and (2) maintaining high utilization across workloads that dynamically shift between POLY and EC stages. Existing reconfigurable accelerators address these issues only partially, remaining limited in precision scalability, algorithmic flexibility, and resource efficiency.\nL18: > To overcome these limitations, we propose ZK-Flex, a flexible and scalable software-hardware co-designed framework for accelerating ZKP proof generation. The software layer incorporates POLY and EC optimizers that reduce computation through hardware- and workload-aware algorithmic choices, while the hardware integrates TCore, a Toom-Cook-based multi-precision core with a flexible NoC and a linked-list memory mechanism that improves parallelism under limited memory capacity.\nL19: Across representative ZKP benchmarks, ZK-Flex achieves 5 to 11 times speedup and up to 3.8 times higher area efficiency over the state of the art, establishing a new foundation for high-performance, reconfigurable ZKP acceleration.\nL20: Comments:  | 7 pages, 8 figures, 2 tables. Accepted at DAC 2026 (63rd ACM/IEEE Design Automation Conference), July 26-29, 2026, Long Beach, CA, USA\nL21: Subjects:  | Hardware Architecture (cs.AR); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\nL22: Cite as:  | 【11†arXiv:2606.03046】 [cs.AR]\nL23:    | (or 【12†arXiv:2606.03046v1】 [cs.AR] for this version)\nL24:    | 【13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.03046†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | 【14†https://doi.org/10.1145/3770743.3803941†doi.org】 DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Adiwena Putra [【15†view email】]\nL29: [v1] Tue, 2 Jun 2026 02:30:04 UTC (1,214 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled ZK-Flex: A Flexible and Scalable Framework for Accelerating Zero-Knowledge Proofs, by Adiwena Putra and 3 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * 【9†View PDF】\nL38:   * 【10†HTML (experimental)】\nL39:   * 【16†TeX Source】\nL40: \nL41: 【17†view license†creativecommons.org】\nL42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.AR\nL45: \nL46: 【18†< prev】   |   【19†next >】\nL47: \nL48: 【20†new】 | 【21†recent】 | 【22†2026-06】\nL49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: 【23†cs】\nL53: 【24†cs.CR】\nL54: \nL55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * 【25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu】\nL58:   * 【26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL5